  {
   "rule": "A noun's ending changes with its <strong>job</strong> in the sentence. This change is called <em>iʿrāb</em>. Same word, three jobs: <span class='ar raf'>الْبَابُ</span> مَفْتُوحٌ (subject) · فَتَحْتُ <span class='ar nasb'>الْبَابَ</span> (object) · عَلَى <span class='ar jarr'>الْبَابِ</span> (after a preposition)."
  },
  {
   "h": "Words that never change (mabnī)"
  },
  {
   "p": "Some words keep the same ending whatever their job. They're called <em>mabnī</em>: <span class='ar'>هَذَا</span>, <span class='ar'>ذَلِكَ</span>, <span class='ar'>مَنْ</span>, <span class='ar'>مَا</span>, <span class='ar'>أَيْنَ</span>, <span class='ar'>هُوَ</span>, <span class='ar'>أَنَا</span> and the past-tense verb <span class='ar'>ذَهَبَ</span>. Words that do change are <em>muʿrab</em>."
  },
  {
   "tip": "This table is the backbone of all Arabic grammar. Stages 2 and 3 add more jobs to each row, but the three cases never change."
  }
